          %p
            The
            %strong de Rham theorem
            (named after
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+de+Rham"} Georges de Rham
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi dR
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^n_{dR}(X)
            of a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (without boundary) is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+cohomology"} real cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^n(X, \mathbb{R})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+cohomology"} Čech cohomology
            with real coefficients.
          %p
            The theorem has several dozens of different proofs. For example in the Čech approach one can make a double complex whose first row is the Čech complex of a covering and first column is the de Rham complex and other entries are mixed and use spectral sequence argument (cf.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BottTu82"} Bott &amp; Tu 1982
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Postnikov89"} Postnikov 1989
          %p
            This is maybe best formulated, understood and proven in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_c
            for
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the abelian group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %li
              %p
                regarded not as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
                with the standard
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
                structure on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                but as a topologically discrete group on the underlying set of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %li
              %p
                and then regarded as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
                on
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+sheaf"} constant sheaf
                that sends connected
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
                to the set of
                %em constant
                maps
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}_c
            for the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+object"} Eilenberg-MacLane object
            in
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_c
            in degree
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}_c
                  \=
                  (\cdots \to 0 \to \mathbb{R}_c \to 0 \to \cdots \to 0)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Next, write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi n
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar \mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}
            (without the subscript
            = succeed "!)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne complex
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi n
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi dR
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi dR
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi dR
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi dR
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi closed
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bar \mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}
                  \=
                  (C^\infty(-,\mathbb{R}) \stackrel{d_{dR}}{\to}
                  \\Omega^1(-) \stackrel{d_{dR}}{\to}
                  \\Omega^2(-) \stackrel{d_{dR}}{\to} \cdots \stackrel{d_{dR}}{\to} \Omega^n_{closed}(-))
                  \\,.
          %p
            (The notation here is borrowed from that used at
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+sheaves%2C+cohomology+and+higher+stacks"} motivation for sheaves, cohomology and higher stacks
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi n
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar \mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}
            as a differential refinement of the object
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}_c
          %p Then we have:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                “ordinary”
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
                with coefficients in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi n
                        %msub
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}_c
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
                with coefficients in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mover
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                        %mi n
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar \mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}
                (this is semi-obvious, requires a bit more discussion).
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+lemma"} Poincare lemma
                says that every closed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
                is locally exact, and hence there is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
                of chain complexes of sheaves
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %msub
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi c
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo ≃
                      %msup
                        %mover
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                        %mi n
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}_c \stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
                      \\bar \mathbf{B}^n \mathbb{R}
              %p
                given by injecting for each
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
                the set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                as the constant functions into
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(U,\mathbb{R})
          %p
            It is this quasi-isomorphism of coefficient objects that induces the de Rham isomorphism of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi dR
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^n(X,\mathbb{R}) \simeq
                  H^n_{dR}(X)
                  \\,.
          %p …
          %h3#OnCochains On cochains
          %p
            The equivalence on cohomology asserted by the de Rham theorem is but a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
            of a more refined statement: a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
            of
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} equivalence
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+algebra"} A-infinity algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\Omega^\bullet(X), d_{dR})
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
                  (C(X), \cup)
          %p
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            and the collection of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cochain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Gugenheim"}> Gugenheim, 1977
            ).
          %p
            Furthermore, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-infinity+algebra"} E-infinity algebra
            structure on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            (trivially induced by the
            %span.newWikiWord
              commutative dga
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/commutative+dga"} ?
            structure) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cochains"} singular cochains
            (as witnessed by the action of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence+operad"} sequence operad
            of McClure and Smith on
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cochains"} singular cochains
          %h3#SyntheticVersion Synthetic version
          %p
            The de Rham theorem also holds internally in the context of suitable
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            modelling the axioms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
          %p Specifically
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the internal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular chain complex
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
                is given as the
                = succeed "-linear" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed "-module" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                objects
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta^n,X]
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is the internal incarnation of the real numbers;
            %li
              %p
                the de Rham complex is given by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms+in+synthetic+differential+geometry"} differential forms in synthetic differential geometry
          %p
            The de Rham theorem in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            then asserts that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a manifold regarded as an object in the well-adapted smooth topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            the morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mo ∫
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\textstyle{\int} \;\colon\; H^p(X) \to H_p(X,R)^*
          %p
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in \mathbb{N}
          %p This is discussed in chapter IV of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} Models for Smooth Infinitesimal Analysis
              for the toposes
